+# 1. Purpose
+
+This document defines architectural requirements for extending and evolving Document Identity under applicable common Architecture/Policy and responsible domain contracts. It distinguishes ordinary compatible extension from necessary assessed normative/foundational revision. An extension label does not authorize changing owner meaning or prove backward compatibility.
+
+Scope each proposal to actual intended subjects/domains, governing source editions and affected consumers. Applicable assigned normalized logical documents, pre-allocation subjects, other entity keys, physical files, exact editions/status and mutable supporting records are distinct. Every README.md, CHANGELOG.md and ArchitecturalMilestones.md remains non-normalized at every location; no identity is allocated merely because a file, category, representation or extension exists.
+
+This source specifies documentary contracts and evidence boundaries, not implementation mechanisms, operational permissions, actual allocation/domain registration, installed adapters or a complete required graph. A local source correction/validation does not establish integrated conformity, formal architectural approval, publication or exact Baseline inclusion.
+
+---
+
+# 2. Introduction
+
+The six original fundamental property subjects remain:
+
+- uniqueness;
+- permanence;
+- immutability;
+- stability;
+- canonical representation;
+- implementation independence.
+
+Apply these to the governing assigned qualified Domain+Identifier key and same-document association, actual canonical interpretation and responsible contracts. They do not freeze every state/reference/descriptor/registration/projection record or equate files, exact editions and other entities with new document identities. Copies/aliases/editions or equal numeric suffixes in different series are not alone competing allocations; actual assignment/canonicalization/population/reservation/history and coverage determine the claim.
+
+Ordinary compatible extensions preserve applicable owner meaning and assigned-key continuity. Necessary changes to normative/foundational contracts require §9 prior/new authority, rationale, owner/consumer/interface, compatibility, history and transition assessment. Preserve allocated/retired non-reuse and historical reference/exact-snapshot continuity; no arbitrary reassignment to another document or silent historical retargeting is permitted. Supporting records may evolve under their owners without key reallocation.
+
+Actual mandatory State/Canonical Reference and complete-record/lifecycle authority in DID-005/006/014 remain pending reconciliation; a selected key view does not remove them or invent an accepted merged schema. This UPDATE executes no identity mutation, allocation, retirement or migration.
+
+---
+
+# 3. Extensibility Objectives
+
+The six original objectives remain with explicit evidence limits.
+
+| Original objective | Scoped requirement / evidence boundary |
+|---|---|
+| accommodate future architectural requirements | Identify actual need, alternatives, responsible owners and affected contracts before proposing capability orsource changes |
+| support new identity domains | Require explicit domain qualification/authority/allocation and consumer contracts; do not silently move assigned keys |
+| enable framework evolution | Distinguish ordinary extension from necessary assessed normative revision with prior/new traceability |
+| preserve interoperability | Assess actual provider/consumer/adapter meanings, exact interfaces and transition evidence; no universal rollout inferred |
+| maintain architectural consistency | Evaluate identified exact sources/requirements and scope; retain unresolved mandatory/required-graph authority |
+| guarantee long-term compatibility | Received objective is a design goal and preservation obligation under declared contracts, not an unconditional guarantee for every future consumer orlive service |
+
+For any compatibility/conformance claim record exact subjects, domains, source/rule editions, artifacts/bytes/context, actual executor, checks, observations, coverage and limits. Labels, added capabilities and Version numbers alone prove no outcome.
+
+---
+
+# 4. Extensibility Principles
+
+The five original principle headings remain. They guide ordinary compatible extensions and separately assessed necessary normative revision under common governing contracts; no new AP identifiers are allocated here.
+
+---
+
+## 4.1. Backward Compatibility
+
+Ordinary compatible extensions shall preserve the applicable meanings and assigned same-document key association of existing identities under their governing contracts. Compatibility requires actual prior/new provider/consumer/representation/reference assessment, not merely unchanged names or an additive label.
+
+A necessary incompatible normative revision must follow §9 with explicit responsible authority, rationale/alternatives, affected exact owners/consumers/interfaces, mappings/history, Version/Revision and transition checks. It is not silently treated as a compatible extension. Preserve allocated/retired reservations, intended historical interpretation and immutable snapshot members; no arbitrary key reassignment or universal guarantee of live reference validity follows.
+
+---
+
+## 4.2. Canonical Preservation
+
+An ordinary implementation extension shall not silently redefine responsible canonical document-key/domain semantics. Document Identity remains the semantic owner under applicable common Architecture/Policy, while other descriptor/category/connection/registration/projection owners retain their contracts.
+
+The canonical model is an architectural contract, distinct from mutable support records and runtime representations. Necessary assessed normative/foundational revision follows common evolution principles and §9; core definition is not made unchangeable for all future governed editions merely by calling it canonical.
+
+DID-014§14 still states additive/backward-compatible-only extensions and that core identity semantics are not extensible. Preserve that actual peer restriction and its pending owner/authority reconciliation with the controlled-evolution approach; this source-local correction neither waives it nor claims universal agreement or implementation permission.
+
+---
+
+## 4.3. Non-Destructive Evolution
+
+Prefer additive, compatible evolution where it meets the actual need without competing semantics or unnecessary disruption. Preserve original evidence, assignments/reservations, same-document continuity and historical exact-snapshot interpretation.
+
+Additive-only is not an absolute ban on a necessary normative/foundational revision under ARC-002 AP-015–018. Such revision requires §9 prior/new rules, responsible authority, alternatives/rationale, exact owner/consumer/required-interface impact, compatibility/version and transition evidence. No extension may silently invalidate governing assignments, rewrite historic failed checks or claim completed consumer propagation without evidence.
+
+---
+
+## 4.4. Separation of Concerns
+
+Respect actual semantic and record owners under common architecture and declared domains. Identity meaning, descriptors, categories, connections, registrations and graph projections have distinct responsibilities. Required consumption, documentary authority, projection, navigation and execution are separately typed; conceptual separation does not remove indispensable requirements.
+
+An ordinary extension shall not silently transfer ownership or invent new components. A necessary architectural responsibility/interface revision requires explicit prior/new assessed authority, allocation/contract impact, affected sources/consumers and transition evidence under §9; no such transfer is executed here. Governance is an area of four component contracts, not a single new operational owner. Preserve actual reciprocal Metadata/Identity required claims for scope/interface reconciliation; relabelling or projection does not dissolve a genuine required cycle.
+
+---
+
+## 4.5. Technology Independence
+
+The five original independence subjects remain:
+
+- programming languages;
+- storage technologies;
+- serialization formats;
+- repositories;
+- implementation frameworks.
+
+Logical document identity meaning shall not be silently redefined by those implementation choices. Explicit governed canonical representation, logical schema/interface and integration constraints remain legitimate; independence does not exempt actual adapters/executors from applicable owner/authority/evidence obligations.
+
+Actual implementations record exact inputs/outputs, mappings/source editions/context, contributors, checks, observations and limitations. A legitimate domain-preserving adapter is distinct from competing implementation-specific identity semantics. No runtime, installed tool, universal format suitability or complete interoperability is certified.
+
+---
+
+# 5. Extensible Elements
+
+All five original extensible subjects remain. Yes denotes a conditional extension subject, not permission, existing implementation or automatic authority to amend canonical semantics. Owner contracts and actual prior/new impact/evidence govern applicability; mandatory fields/rules cannot be removed to make an extension appear compatible.
+
+| Element | Extensible | Qualified conditions / authority boundary |
+|---|---|---|
+| Identity Domain | Yes | Additional explicit governing domain/qualification/owner/consumer contract; not silent reassignment of an existing key; actual uniqueness/reservation scope |
+| Identifier Representation | Yes | Preserve intended qualified identity/canonical interpretation; traceable aliases/mappings and actual interface compatibility; necessary contract revision follows §9 |
+| Validation Rules | Yes | Responsible rule owner and exact claim; additional checks preserve applicable mandatory meanings/outcome/coverage; no silent waiver orfabricated validity |
+| Governance Policies | Yes | Applicable decision/transition/evidence authority and common rules; component contracts distinct from actual DMS/tool/manual/CMS execution and permissions |
+| Reference Mechanisms | Yes | Intended existing qualified document/exact-edition target and context, typed other endpoints and connection ownership; observed historical/live validity limits |
+
+All four original non-extensible subjects remain as constraints on ordinary implementation extensions. This does not assert that responsible normative definitions can never undergo a necessary assessed architectural revision. It also does not make every support record immutable or waive DID-014's actual restrictive peer contract.
+
+| Element | Reason / qualified ordinary-extension boundary |
+|---|---|
+| Identity Permanence | Preserve assigned same-document association, reservations/history and intended interpretation; not perpetual content availability |
+| Identity Uniqueness | Preserve full qualified governing assignment/canonicalization contract with actual population/collision/history evidence |
+| Identity Immutability | Preserve assigned key/association; mutable support records retain their own responsible rules; necessary contract transition requires §9 |
+| Canonical Identity Model | Ordinary implementation extensions do not silently replace owner meaning; necessary normative/foundational revision requires actual prior/new authority/impact/version/transition assessment and visible peer reconciliation |
+
+DID-014§§7–8 mandatory State/Canonical Reference and DID-006 lifecycle authority remain actual owner work, not fields silently waived by these tables. No accepted merged model, Metadata Element/Profile, new domain, series, alias or allocation is created by an extensibility label.
+
+---
+
+# 6. Extension Categories
+
+The four original extension categories are conceptual groupings, not additional document Type values, registered components or automatic allocation to similarly named structural areas. Preserve their received titles, seven examples and actual owner/consumer boundaries. Examples indicate possible subjects, not implemented adapters or authorized operations.
+
+---
+
+## 6.1. Architectural Extensions
+
+Architectural extensions introduce demonstrated concepts/contracts while preserving compatible meaning where feasible. The original example remains:
+
+- additional identity domains.
+
+New domains require explicit governing authority, qualified semantics, canonical representation, allocation/reservation scope and affected consumer contracts. They are not a shortcut to renumber an existing document. For this corpus the domain is Documentation Framework, represented by DF; DF-SSS-NNN uses a registered uppercase three-letter stable allocation series and positive three-digit 001–999. 000 is not allocated; gaps prove no availability; allocated/retired numbers are not reused. At999, register a traceable unused continuation series rather than widen syntax or recycle. Current Family/Type/Component/path/status/edition does not reconstruct the key. Other domains require their own explicit contracts.
+
+A necessary change to an existing architectural contract is assessed under §9, not assumed harmless because it is called an additional domain. This UPDATE registers no domain/series and allocates no key.
+
+---
+
+## 6.2. Behavioural Extensions
+
+The received heading Behavioural Extensions denotes additional validation/governance behavior, not a new normalized Type value. Its original example remains:
+
+- organisation-specific validation policies.
+
+Actual responsible owners define applicable additional checks or policy conditions under common governing contracts. Record exact subject/rules/editions/context, authority/executor, performed checks, observations and coverage. DID-007 distinguishes Valid, Invalid, Inconsistent and Unresolvable; partial/unexecuted/missing/conflicting mandatory evidence cannot establish unconditional full validity or fabricated failure. An additional policy does not silently redefine mandatory semantics, create permissions ormerge unresolved State/Canonical Reference/lifecycle fields.
+
+DMS/tool/manual operation and CMS change scope are distinct from Workflow/Policies/Reviews/Baselines definitions. A result record or local PASS does not authorize allocation, correction, approval, publication orBaseline selection.
+
+---
+
+## 6.3. Integration Extensions
+
+Integration extensions describe possible external exchange/adapter contracts. The two original examples remain:
+
+- external identifier mappings;
+- repository-specific adapters.
+
+Map intended qualified keys, canonical interpretations/aliases and exact editions/context with actual prior/new source/output provenance, owner authority and consumer compatibility. Repository locators and external strings are not automatically canonical document keys. Preserve reservations/history and immutable snapshot targets; no silent allocation, domain reassignment orretargeting is allowed.
+
+Use applicable registered Integration capabilities from ARC-005§9 when relevant:Source Control Integration, CI/CD Integration, IDE Integration, Documentation Platform Integration and AI Integration. The two examples are not new registered components orproof that any adapter exists. Actual execution requires applicable authorization, mappings, input/output editions/bytes, contributors, checks, observations and limits; generation/exchange cannot invent identity, state, approval orcontribution facts.
+
+---
+
+## 6.4. Implementation Extensions
+
+Implementation extensions realize applicable logical contracts without acquiring their semantic ownership. The three original examples remain:
+
+- programming language libraries;
+- database mappings;
+- API integrations.
+
+Their implementation design remains outside this specification. That exclusion does not waive logical representation/interface, owner/authorization or evidence obligations. Actual implementations preserve intended qualified identity/edition/context, mappings/source provenance, contributors and applicable checks; storage/runtime choices are not proof of correctness orindependent consumer rollout.
+
+Architectural capability, actual adapter/executor, generated output and observed result remain distinct. No language library, database model, API service, universal resolver orpermission model is supplied here.
+
+---
+
+# 7. Extension Constraints
+
+The seven original DID-E labels remain source-local extension constraints, not normalized document allocations or newly invented global registrations. Apply them to declared subjects/operations under responsible contracts; they do not grant a universal permission or automatic compatibility verdict.
+
+---
+
+## 7.1. DID-E-001
+
+Preserve existing assigned qualified document-key/same-document association and historical interpretation under its governing contract. Canonical labeling alone establishes neither assignment evidence norfull validity of every record/reference. Mandatory field/state/lifecycle authority and coverage remain explicit; no arbitrary reassignment orsilent historical retargeting is permitted.
+
+---
+
+## 7.2. DID-E-002
+
+Preserve existing assigned identifiers and canonical interpretation across ordinary evolution. Current Family/Type/location/format/status/edition changes do not regenerate stable allocation series. Necessary representation/domain/contract transitions require§9 prior/new mapping, authority, owner/consumer, aliases/reservations/collisions/history and intended reference/exact-snapshot continuity; no reuse ofallocated/retired keys for another document.
+
+---
+
+## 7.3. DID-E-003
+
+Assess affected actual architectural relationships and preserve compatible required contracts where feasible. Do not promise that every previous relationship remains valid forever. Typed document endpoints require intended qualified document/exact-edition/context; other endpoint kinds retain their own contracts. Connection records remain with Relationships, registrations with Registry and projections with Knowledge Graph.
+
+Incoming references may be0..N; optional incoming population does not waive validity of each actual reference. Observe dangling, ambiguous, stale or unavailable targets, alias interpretation and current versus historical context. Stable identity does not guarantee perpetual live retrieval orcomplete connection validity. Necessary contract change follows §9 with actual owner/consumer and required-interface evidence; never silently create orretarget akey orimmutable historical member to make acheck pass.
+
+---
+
+## 7.4. DID-E-004
+
+Preserve full qualified Domain+Identifier uniqueness under its declared canonicalization/allocation contract with actual assignment, population, current/historical collision/reservation evidence and coverage. Copies, legitimate aliases, projections, exact editions and equal suffixes across different series do not alone constitute competing allocations. Missing history/index entries orsyntax success prove neither availability norcomplete uniqueness.
+
+---
+
+## 7.5. DID-E-005
+
+Preserve assigned same-document association, allocated/retired reservations and intended historical recognition under responsible contracts. Retirement needs actual decision/scope and history; document Status orfile/edition/index deletion alone does not retire the logical identity orgrant a missing lifecycle edge. Permanence does not freeze support records orpromise perpetual content availability.
+
+---
+
+## 7.6. DID-E-006
+
+Preserve assigned qualified key and same-document association during ordinary evolution. Supporting state/reference/descriptor/registration/projection records may legitimately evolve under their owners. Before necessary governing-contract/representation change require§9 prior/new authority, compatibility, mappings/history/reference/exact-snapshot continuity and later exact validation; no arbitrary key mutation, reassignment orhistorical evidence rewrite is authorized.
+
+---
+
+## 7.7. DID-E-007
+
+An ordinary extension shall not silently redefine Document Identity's document-key/domain ownership under applicable common Architecture/Policy. Descriptor/category/connection/registration/projection owners retain their meaning and records. A necessary architectural responsibility/interface revision requires explicit prior/new assessed authority, scope, affected exact owners/consumers and transition evidence under §9; no such transfer is performed here.
+
+Semantic ownership is not sole authority overevery rule, operational permission orabsence of actual required dependencies. Preserve reciprocal Metadata/Identity claims and mandatory peer authority visibly; changing a relationship label orcalling data a projection cannot remove an indispensable contract.
+
+---
